{"type":"event","event":{"type":"message_update","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now let me run the public test and a comprehensive inline test suite. I'll write a test script inline via heredoc to avoid creating files, covering:\n\n1. Public test.\n2. Concurrency bound and utilization (max in flight == limit, never exceeds).\n3. Order preservation with random delays.\n4. Any iterable: list, tuple, set, dict, range, generator, async generator.\n5. limit < 1 (0, -1, -100) → ValueError.\n6. Laziness: pulls bounded at first fn call; infinite generator + error stops it; number of created tasks bounded.\n7. Exception propagation, no lingering tasks / no warnings.\n8. Empty iterable, limit=1.\n9. Outer cancellation cleanup.\n10. fn result order with uneven delays.\n11. Large-ish generator to ensure no eager task creation (track max pending via all_tasks?).\n\nLet me write one comprehensive script via bash heredoc and run with python3 -W error::RuntimeWarning maybe. Actually warnings for unretrieved exceptions appear as \"Task exception was never retrieved\" logged, not warnings. Let me capture with a custom exception handler.\n\nLet me write the test script carefully.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, random, sys\nsys.path.insert(0, '/tmp/flashbench_deepseek_v41/t07')\nfrom amap import bounded_map\n\n# 1 public\nasync def f(x): return x*2\nassert asyncio.run(bounded_map(f, [1,2,3], 2)) == [2,4,6]\n\n# 2 concurrency bound\ndef run_limit(items, limit, delays):\n    active = 0\n    max_active = 0\n    calls = 0\n    async def fn(x):\n        nonlocal active, max_active, calls\n        calls += 1\n        active += 1\n        max_active = max(max_active, active)\n        try:\n            await asyncio.sleep(delays[x % len(delays)])\n            return x\n        finally:\n            active -= 1\n    res = asyncio.run(bounded_map(fn, items, limit))\n    return res, max_active, calls\n\nres, mx, calls = run_limit(list(range(50)), 5, [0.01, 0.001, 0.005])\nassert res == list(range(50))\nassert mx == 5, mx\nassert calls == 50\n\n# uneven delays order\nres, mx, calls = run_limit(list(range(12)), 3, [0.02, 0.0, 0.005, 0.001])\nassert res == list(range(12)), res\nassert mx == 3\n\n# 3 iterables\nasync def double(x): return x*2\nassert asyncio.run(bounded_map(double, (1,2,3), 2)) == [2,4,6]\nassert asyncio.run(bounded_map(double, range(5), 2)) == [0,2,4,6,8]\ns = {1,2,3}\nassert asyncio.run(bounded_map(double, s, 2)) == [x*2 for x in s]\nd = {'a':1,'b':2}\nassert asyncio.run(bounded_map(lambda k: k.upper() if False else double(1), d, 2)) ... \n```\n\nHmm, for dict, fn receives keys. Let me define async fn for that.\n\n```python\nasync def ident(x): return x\nassert asyncio.run(bounded_map(ident, (i for i in range(6)), 2)) == list(range(6))\n```\n\nWait ident returns coroutine; but need await? `async def ident(x): return x` is fine.\n\nSet order: iterate set, map, compare with [x*2 for x in s] — same iteration order as iter(s), and our function iterates once. Yes.\n\nAsync generator:\n```python\nasync def agen():\n    for i in range(5):\n        yield i\nassert asyncio.run(bounded_map(ident, agen(), 2)) == list(range(5))\n```\n\n# 4 limit validation\n```python\nfor bad in (0, -1, -5):\n    try:\n        asyncio.run(bounded_map(ident, [1,2], bad))\n    except ValueError:\n        pass\n    else:\n        raise AssertionError(f\"no ValueError for {bad}\")\n```\n\nAlso check the ValueError is raised even with an empty iterable and that fn is never called.\n\n# 5 laziness\n```python\npulls = 0\nactive = 0\nmax_active = 0\nasync def slow(x):\n    nonlocal active, max_active\n    active += 1\n    max_active = max(max_active, active)\n    await asyncio.sleep(0.01)\n    active -= 1\n    return x\n\ndef gen():\n    nonlocal pulls\n    i = 0\n    while True:\n        pulls += 1\n        yield i\n        i += 1\n```\n\nTo test laziness of infinite iterable, fn raises at some point:\n```python\nclass Boom(Exception): pass\ncreated = 0\nasync def fail_on_10(x):\n    nonlocal created\n    created += 1\n    if x == 10: raise Boom\n    await asyncio.sleep(0.001)\n    return x\ntry:\n    asyncio.run(bounded_map(fail_on_10, gen(), 3))\nexcept Boom:\n    pass\n```\nAfter: pulls ≤ created + limit? pulls could be up to created + limit. Assert pulls < 50 and created < 50 — proves not eager. Also assert pulls == created + something small. Actually with limit=3: fill pulls 3 (0,1,2); complete→pull 3; ... it will keep up. created reaches 10, then Boom. pulls maybe 11-13. Fine.\n\nAlso check at first call, pulls ≤ limit:\n```python\nseen = []\nasync def probe(x):\n    seen.append(pulls)\n    return x\ninf = gen2()  # fresh counter\ntry:\n    asyncio.run(bounded_map(probe, inf, 4))\nexcept ...\n```\nHmm, infinite generator would never finish. Use generator of 100 items with a counter; at the time of the first call, pulls ≤ limit. Actually fill consumes limit items before first wait, so pulls == limit exactly when fn starts (fn starts in task after ensure_future, and fill continues consuming until pending==limit). Order: fill submits task0 (task doesn't run yet — created via ensure_future, scheduled), then pulls item1, submits, ..., until limit tasks. Then `await fill()` — the await of pull() on a sync iterator doesn't yield to loop, so tasks don't run until `asyncio.wait` yields. So at first fn call, pulls == limit. Then subsequent pulls happen as completions free slots: pulls ≤ completed + limit. So assert pulls - completed <= limit at all times. I can track inside fn: `assert pulls - completed_count <= limit`. Nice invariant. \n\nLet me do:\n```python\npulls = 0\ncompleted = 0\nviolations = 0\nlimit = 4\ndef gen():\n    nonlocal pulls\n    i = 0\n    while i < 200:\n        pulls += 1\n        yield i\n        i += 1\nasync def fn(x):\n    nonlocal completed, violations\n    if pulls - completed > limit:\n        violations += 1\n    await asyncio.sleep(0.001)\n    completed += 1\n    return x\nres = asyncio.run(bounded_map(fn, gen(), limit))\nassert res == list(range(200))\nassert violations == 0\n```\n\nHmm, is `pulls - completed <= limit` always true? pulls counts consumed; each consumed becomes a pending task, so pending = pulls - completed. Yes invariant. Good.\n\nAlso track max concurrent tasks inside fn using active counter == pending; assert active ≤ limit.\n\n# 6 error propagation & cleanup\n```python\nclass Boom(Exception): pass\nasync def boom(x):\n    if x == 3: raise Boom(\"3\")\n    await asyncio.sleep(0.01)\n    return x\n\ntry:\n    asyncio.run(bounded_map(boom, range(100), 4))\nexcept Boom:\n    pass\nelse:\n    raise AssertionError\n```\n\nAlso check no pending tasks left after error: run within a loop and check all_tasks at end; after asyncio.run returns, loop closed so tasks should be done. We can check inside:\n\n```python\nasync def main():\n    try:\n        await bounded_map(boom, range(100), 4)\n    except Boom:\n        pass\n    await asyncio.sleep(0)\n    return [t for t in asyncio.all_tasks() if t is not asyncio.current_task()]\nts = asyncio.run(main())\nassert ts == [], ts\n```\n\nAlso ensure no unretrieved exceptions; set custom exception handler that records context 'Task exception was never retrieved'.\n\n```python\nerrors = []\nloop = asyncio.new_event_loop()\nloop.set_exception_handler(lambda loop, ctx: errors.append(ctx))\nasyncio.set_event_loop(loop)\nloop.run_until_complete(...)\nloop.close()\nassert errors == []\n```\n\nHmm, the error handler is called for various things; with our design no unretrieved task exceptions should occur because we cancel and gather with return_exceptions=True.\n\n# 7 caller cancellation\n```python\nstarted = 0\ncancelled = 0\nasync def slow(x):\n    nonlocal started, cancelled\n    started += 1\n    try:\n        await asyncio.sleep(10)\n    except asyncio.CancelledError:\n        cancelled += 1\n        raise\n    return x\n\nasync def main():\n    t = asyncio.ensure_future(bounded_map(slow, range(50), 3))\n    await asyncio.sleep(0.05)\n    t.cancel()\n    try:\n        await t\n    except asyncio.CancelledError:\n        pass\n    await asyncio.sleep(0)\n    leftover = [x for x in asyncio.all_tasks() if x is not asyncio.current_task()]\n    return started, cancelled, leftover\nstarted, cancelled, leftover = asyncio.run(main())\nassert started == 3, started\nassert cancelled == 3, cancelled\nassert leftover == []\n```\n\n# 8 empty & limit 1\n```python\nassert asyncio.run(bounded_map(ident, [], 3)) == []\nres, mx, _ = run_limit(list(range(10)), 1, [0.001])\nassert res == list(range(10)) and mx == 1\n```\n\n# 9 results order with random delays: done above.\n\nLet me now run the public test first, then the comprehensive script.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Now let me run the public test, then a comprehensive inline suite covering concurrency bounds, ordering with uneven delays, iterable types, laziness on infinite iterables, error propagation, and cancellation cleanup."}]}}
